| Apache-2.0 | requests 2.32 |\n| MIT | left-pad 0.2 |\n| GPL-3.0 | somelib 1.1 |\n\n**Copyleft flag: `somelib` 1.1 (GPL-3.0) is a problem for a closed-source product.**\n\n- GPL-3.0 is strong copyleft. If you distribute a product that links to or bundles `somelib`, the whole combined work generally has to be released under GPL-3.0. That means providing source and licensing it under GPL terms, which conflicts with keeping it closed.\n- GPL-3.0 only applies on distribution. A hosted service the user never receives a copy of usually doesn't trigger it. Desktop apps, mobile apps, on-prem installs, and shipped containers do.\n- GPL-3.0 also has anti-tivoization and patent-licensing terms, so it's stricter than GPL-2.0.\n\n**Options**\n1. Replace `somelib` with a permissively licensed alternative. This is usually the simplest fix.\n2. Ask the author for a commercial or alternative license.\n3. Isolate it as a separate process that talks over a CLI or network boundary. Have counsel review this first, because the line is fuzzy.\n4. Drop the feature that needs it.\n5. Release your product under GPL-3.0, if that's acceptable.\n\n**The permissive ones are fine**\n- `requests` (Apache-2.0) and `left-pad` (MIT) allow closed-source use.\n- Keep their license and copyright notices in your attributions or NOTICE file.\n- Apache-2.0 also requires you to include its NOTICE file contents, if any, and to state changes you made to the code.\n\n**Caveats**\n- I only used the license labels in `deps.txt`. I didn't check the packages themselves. Confirm that `somelib`'s license really is GPL-3.0 and not \"GPL with linking exception\" or a dual license.\n- This list has no transitive dependencies. They can bring in other licenses, so run a scan over the full resolved tree.\n- This is not legal advice. Have counsel sign off on the GPL question before you ship.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}